WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.080
When you're ready to move up to the major leagues, you're ready for RBI Baseball.

00:00:20.240 --> 00:00:28.020
And welcome to NEStalgia, a chronological exploration of every NES game released in North America.

00:00:28.020 --> 00:00:29.100
I'm Mike.

00:00:29.360 --> 00:00:31.160
I'm Joe. And I'm Sean.

00:00:31.680 --> 00:00:33.420
Alright. Sean, don't worry.

00:00:33.640 --> 00:00:38.460
Even though you're the last one to say your name, you'll be the first one to talk after me just now as I'm speaking.

00:00:38.900 --> 00:00:43.040
Yep, you will. Because you have a very important job for us.

00:00:43.200 --> 00:00:44.120
Yes, what is this?

00:00:44.360 --> 00:00:50.460
Nobody here has ever played a baseball game before other than NES Baseball and...

00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:54.920
What was the other name of the other baseball game?

00:00:54.920 --> 00:00:56.460
No, it was Major League Baseball.

00:00:56.500 --> 00:00:57.640
Major League Baseball. Major League Baseball.

00:00:57.640 --> 00:00:59.560
That was our 100th episode not too long ago.

00:00:59.900 --> 00:01:03.980
Right. If you can't tell by now, we're joking that we haven't played baseball before.

00:01:04.180 --> 00:01:09.620
And we're pretty into baseball as a thing, but we only had one baseball game for like a really long time.

00:01:09.620 --> 00:01:11.760
And it was our second game we ever reviewed.

00:01:11.920 --> 00:01:18.040
It's episode two. And then we went 98 episodes before we ever saw another baseball game.

00:01:18.040 --> 00:01:31.240
But now they're coming in like crazy because we're hitting that peak start of the season time of June 1988.

00:01:31.620 --> 00:01:34.240
So it's in full effect now.

00:01:34.400 --> 00:01:36.000
The season started earlier than that.

00:01:36.140 --> 00:01:37.660
It started in late March.

00:01:37.660 --> 00:01:41.240
But, you know, this is the time to be releasing your baseball games.

00:01:41.480 --> 00:01:54.020
Yeah, I think that while we have played these baseball games pretty often now, I would say that, you know, sometimes it's hard to keep track of the rules.

00:01:54.300 --> 00:02:00.260
You know, like, I don't know if I can remember what baseball is, but I could try.

00:02:00.260 --> 00:02:03.640
Could you tell us what baseball is?

00:02:03.900 --> 00:02:15.540
Yeah, yeah. In case of in case any of our listeners don't remember what baseball is, just as like an overview of the episode.

00:02:16.760 --> 00:02:26.800
Baseball is what happens when 18 people get together and they decide to designate each other as batsmen and pitchmen.

00:02:27.800 --> 00:02:30.880
And the batsmen stand in front of the catchmen.

00:02:31.780 --> 00:02:39.240
And what happens then is the pitchmen will stand on a tall hill and throw balls at the batsmen.

00:02:39.240 --> 00:02:40.160
A tall hill?

00:02:40.360 --> 00:02:41.740
A tall hill. Yeah.

00:02:41.920 --> 00:02:44.720
Okay. They throw balls at the batsmen.

00:02:46.140 --> 00:02:51.100
And if the batsmen can hit any of the balls out of the park, they score.

00:02:52.180 --> 00:03:00.700
But they can also choose to hit them inside of the park and stay on the, like, aisles of safety that have been designated on the baseball diamonds.

00:03:00.900 --> 00:03:03.780
There are islands of safety in the archipelago of the bases.

00:03:04.700 --> 00:03:09.220
And you can only walk along certain shipping lanes to get to them.

00:03:09.720 --> 00:03:17.640
Really, baseball is, like, you know, because it's the great American sport, it has a lot of ties back to the American Revolution.

00:03:18.820 --> 00:03:22.480
I love how straight that was going until that moment.

00:03:22.500 --> 00:03:23.800
I was just thinking about it.

00:03:23.900 --> 00:03:24.900
What I was about to say.

00:03:25.500 --> 00:03:29.300
It really is like the American Revolutionary War in many ways.

00:03:29.480 --> 00:03:33.440
And that's why everybody who's a patriot loves baseball.

00:03:33.800 --> 00:03:36.020
So thank you for that explanation, Sean.

00:03:36.220 --> 00:03:55.380
Yeah. Now, we should probably address the baseball ghost in the room and say, like, what is the difference here between the game that we just played called Major League Baseball and the Nintendo version of baseball and this RBI baseball?

00:03:55.380 --> 00:04:09.560
I think that one reason that it's, like, maybe a little less, it's okay-ish that we're having all these baseball games back to back is that when we had our first baseball game, which was NES baseball, definitely a lot of room for improvement.

00:04:09.860 --> 00:04:12.380
I mean, I don't even think, you couldn't even feel the ball in that one, right?

00:04:12.540 --> 00:04:13.460
No, it just happened.

00:04:13.780 --> 00:04:16.520
I mean, it was like a whole half of the game you had no control over.

00:04:16.520 --> 00:04:20.420
So I think that it's, we're climbing.

00:04:20.700 --> 00:04:22.880
We're slowly getting better and better.

00:04:23.120 --> 00:04:26.140
But where things are gained, other things are taken away.

00:04:26.400 --> 00:04:33.000
So in Major League Baseball, it was the first of any of our games, really, to have, like, a license attached to it.

00:04:33.100 --> 00:04:36.440
So we actually had actual Major League Baseball teams.

00:04:37.100 --> 00:04:41.500
But they couldn't get any of the players because they didn't have the MLB.

00:04:41.720 --> 00:04:43.060
But they got their stats. Yes, they did.

00:04:43.060 --> 00:04:45.980
So you had to kind of figure out what you were.

00:04:46.100 --> 00:04:48.480
Yeah, you had to figure out who it was. You couldn't use their likeliness, you know.

00:04:48.800 --> 00:04:50.980
I guess you could use their likeliness because those are their stats.

00:04:51.040 --> 00:04:52.360
But you couldn't use their names. Yes.

00:04:52.580 --> 00:04:56.680
And they didn't have the Major League Baseball Players Association signed on.

00:04:56.840 --> 00:05:04.080
Now, in this game, RBI Baseball, they decided to go make a deal with the Players Association, but not with Major League Baseball.

00:05:04.180 --> 00:05:06.520
So you're playing with made-up teams.

00:05:06.520 --> 00:05:11.060
And I say that very lightly because they're definitely respective to the teams that they're on.

00:05:11.060 --> 00:05:13.800
Like, Daryl Strawberry is on the New York team.

00:05:14.040 --> 00:05:18.500
Mm-hmm. But there's no New York Mets.

00:05:18.700 --> 00:05:20.300
There's no Philadelphia Phillies.

00:05:20.400 --> 00:05:21.820
There's no San Francisco Giants.

00:05:22.080 --> 00:05:23.220
Nothing is lost.

00:05:23.520 --> 00:05:28.520
Nothing's lost in losing the names of the teams as opposed to losing the names of the players.

00:05:28.540 --> 00:05:29.780
I think this is the better deal.

00:05:29.900 --> 00:05:34.800
If you have to live in a world where they got split up between two games, I think this is the better deal.

00:05:34.880 --> 00:05:36.360
Because it's like, you know the teams.

00:05:36.620 --> 00:05:39.400
It's harder to figure out, wait, which player is that unless they just like know all their stats.

00:05:39.400 --> 00:05:47.620
But would you argue, as sports fans that we are, that you're really only ever rooting for the team name and not necessarily the players,

00:05:47.640 --> 00:05:50.680
and that you hate some of those people the second they go to a team you don't like?

00:05:50.820 --> 00:05:53.860
I think it's easy to map the team name onto the team that you know it is.

00:05:53.860 --> 00:06:04.180
The fact that in Major League Baseball there were no logos and there was no difference between the presentation of playing at Stadium A or Stadium B,

00:06:04.380 --> 00:06:08.740
I can live without the select screen saying the full name of the team.

00:06:08.840 --> 00:06:09.780
That's a great point.

00:06:09.780 --> 00:06:17.380
And I'm happy to be playing RBI Baseball because I can finally play the 1986 World Series the way it was intended to be.

00:06:17.440 --> 00:06:21.420
I can be the New York Mets because they specifically give you the 86 Mets.

00:06:21.420 --> 00:06:26.940
They actually do a really weird way of choosing the teams that get included into this game because it's not all 30 teams.

00:06:27.140 --> 00:06:32.560
It's actually the best teams from 86 and then the best teams from 87.

00:06:32.560 --> 00:06:35.540
So the four and four to get you eight total teams.

00:06:35.740 --> 00:06:38.900
But it's like weird because you're mixing seasons.

00:06:39.200 --> 00:06:41.280
You know, you have like some great players from 87.

00:06:41.420 --> 00:06:46.200
And I wondered, like, was anyone who was great in 86 traded to a great 87 team?

00:06:46.480 --> 00:06:47.940
Yeah, I was just thinking the same thing. But it doesn't happen. There's no carryover.

00:06:48.440 --> 00:06:56.040
I think also because I think the way they did it was it was eight teams and they were division leaders in one of the years.

00:06:56.240 --> 00:06:57.700
It was just one from each division.

00:06:57.860 --> 00:07:01.600
Oh, are you talking about the National League All-Star team?

00:07:02.040 --> 00:07:07.680
I mean, the National League All-Star team and the AL All-Star team are players from other teams.

00:07:07.680 --> 00:07:10.100
From all the other teams that are still considered the best.

00:07:10.100 --> 00:07:15.920
So it wasn't necessarily like the actual full All-Star team lineup because some of the best teams might have those players.

00:07:16.040 --> 00:07:18.880
It was just to include all the other teams, too, which I thought was cool.

00:07:19.080 --> 00:07:20.020
For sure. Yeah.

00:07:20.260 --> 00:07:29.300
I think the biggest thing to note about this game is that this one looks a lot similar to our first Nintendo baseball game.

00:07:29.500 --> 00:07:35.640
Except for it is a lot faster in pace and much more fluid, I would say.

00:07:35.780 --> 00:07:37.360
Yeah, it's more controllable for sure.

00:07:37.360 --> 00:07:50.800
This game definitely, between the similarities in the graphical style and some of the improvements, this looks a lot like our first iterative sports release.

00:07:51.100 --> 00:07:52.800
There's a couple new bells and whistles.

00:07:53.060 --> 00:07:57.240
The baseball will bounce off the grass in this game.

00:07:59.580 --> 00:08:01.100
You can do diving catches, too.

00:08:01.100 --> 00:08:02.380
You can do the diving catch.

00:08:02.520 --> 00:08:09.020
You can't actually put in an input, but if you're far enough away from the ball but still within reach, your player might dive to catch it.

00:08:09.200 --> 00:08:21.620
So there are these small improvements that will continue to happen for an eternity that this game really shows in relation to NES baseball, even though it wasn't made by the same people.

00:08:21.620 --> 00:08:27.440
Yeah, and so if we could just run through the big four, batting, running, pitching, and fielding.

00:08:28.340 --> 00:08:31.920
Batting in this one, how did you feel it was different from the other options we got?

00:08:32.680 --> 00:08:40.960
I feel like, first of all, one thing I noticed right away is that you almost have to move within the batter's box while the pitch is in the air.

00:08:41.040 --> 00:08:44.600
You have to kind of decide where you are as you see the pitch coming.

00:08:44.680 --> 00:08:46.100
Do you guys feel that way? Or did you guys do that?

00:08:46.100 --> 00:08:50.200
Well, you definitely won't make contact with the ball if you're not positioned to hit it.

00:08:50.360 --> 00:08:58.640
Like in this one, it's not so obvious that if you just time the swing with the ball crossing the line that it should be, you'll make contact with it.

00:08:58.700 --> 00:08:59.780
It will not reach.

00:09:00.120 --> 00:09:03.300
Yeah. Right. There are a lot more balls thrown in this game, I feel like.

00:09:03.340 --> 00:09:06.100
Yeah, and that said, yeah, you don't have to swing at every pitch either.

00:09:06.880 --> 00:09:13.380
I definitely moved a lot in the batter's box as well, but I wouldn't say that that actually benefited me in any way.

00:09:13.420 --> 00:09:14.860
I think I probably should have just sat still.

00:09:14.860 --> 00:09:27.960
Yeah. I mean, that could be true for me too, but it just felt, it was just so tempting to often, especially, you know, if it was just like, if, you know, if it was not exactly like fully an outside pitch and you weren't sure and you're like, oh, I'll take a swing at that, whatever, you know.

00:09:28.080 --> 00:09:33.240
But I think that it was, there was a little bit of a learning curve to the timing.

00:09:33.380 --> 00:09:43.400
Once you got it down, it felt very manageable, I think more so than Major League Baseball, where I think I had a little more trouble kind of seeing like, well, when am I hitting it, when am I not?

00:09:43.400 --> 00:09:56.120
But yeah, other than a little bit wider of a, I guess, of a strike zone and a little bit faster, this is pretty much an identical mechanic to both of the other games you've played.

00:09:56.260 --> 00:10:05.940
Totally. Yep. And I would emphasize that fastness too, though, because I definitely appreciated how fast you could get through X amount of innings.

00:10:06.220 --> 00:10:13.900
Because let's be honest, when you're playing these baseball games, you're not in them to actually like think about the, well, how long does it take to watch a baseball game?

00:10:13.980 --> 00:10:16.040
That's how long it should be to play one on the NES.

00:10:16.220 --> 00:10:17.520
It's like, these things should be fast.

00:10:17.640 --> 00:10:21.360
They should be at maximum, maybe 25 minute experience.

00:10:21.360 --> 00:10:23.000
And it's good, because they give you a timer.

00:10:23.280 --> 00:10:28.040
Yeah. Just to make sure that it's under that variable there.

00:10:28.160 --> 00:10:30.080
Right, right. Okay, so that's batting.

00:10:30.840 --> 00:10:32.060
Did anybody hit a home run?

00:10:32.200 --> 00:10:37.600
Because I found it to be relatively easy, but it definitely depended on the person you were batting as, which I thought was nice.

00:10:37.660 --> 00:10:39.360
Yeah, I had a couple. Yeah, one or two.

00:10:39.460 --> 00:10:43.880
But you can definitely tell that there are a difference in the skills of these players.

00:10:43.880 --> 00:10:51.780
At least, while it may not feel it's exactly one-to-one with their stat line, I could feel a difference.

00:10:52.060 --> 00:10:52.860
Got it. Okay.

00:10:53.760 --> 00:11:00.460
And when you hit a home run, too, it's like, the presentation flair that I've always been arguing is missing is still missing here for me.

00:11:00.620 --> 00:11:03.740
Yeah, there's a few, like, immediate instantaneous fireworks.

00:11:04.060 --> 00:11:06.400
Yeah. And then, that's all I needed, though.

00:11:06.400 --> 00:11:07.960
I don't need much more, to be honest.

00:11:08.240 --> 00:11:11.360
And just a billion small little dots that represent all of the fans.

00:11:11.580 --> 00:11:12.900
The billions of fans that go to the game.

00:11:12.900 --> 00:11:15.320
They, like, shake like flies, you know?

00:11:15.460 --> 00:11:18.260
Like, they just get out of control for a home run.

00:11:18.440 --> 00:11:20.060
So, that's nice, I guess.

00:11:21.100 --> 00:11:26.220
Now, with base running, this is where base running and batting kind of merged for me.

00:11:26.300 --> 00:11:27.620
I hated this feature.

00:11:28.240 --> 00:11:30.920
Whenever you hit a ball, it doesn't matter.

00:11:31.160 --> 00:11:32.160
Everybody starts running.

00:11:32.280 --> 00:11:33.580
Everybody runs. It's a hit and run.

00:11:33.800 --> 00:11:35.960
Every single at-bat is a hit and run.

00:11:36.400 --> 00:11:40.860
And not in the true sense that they'll actually start running before you even swing at the ball.

00:11:40.940 --> 00:11:41.820
That's what a hit and run is.

00:11:41.820 --> 00:11:47.200
But here, still, it's so annoying to have to think that you have to control everybody back.

00:11:47.200 --> 00:11:48.140
You have to command everyone to go back.

00:11:48.380 --> 00:11:55.600
If you have more than one man on base, then you're going to have a rough time, like, dealing with fly balls.

00:11:55.640 --> 00:12:01.700
Because you're going to have to press left in A to return the guy back.

00:12:01.840 --> 00:12:03.660
You're going to press up in A to return the guy back.

00:12:03.680 --> 00:12:07.360
It's just a lot to think about while you're also waiting for the ball to land.

00:12:07.360 --> 00:12:18.520
Yeah, and I don't know if this must just be my personal preference, but somehow this game felt more intuitive to me to tell a player to advance.

00:12:19.680 --> 00:12:25.680
Because you use the D-pad to select the player that you want to advance.

00:12:25.680 --> 00:12:30.660
Whereas I think in Major League Baseball, you use the D-pad to select the base you want them to advance to.

00:12:31.160 --> 00:12:32.080
And that was just like one more.

00:12:32.280 --> 00:12:36.700
With having to use the D-pad and then press A and then think about, wait, it was just like one more layer of confusion.

00:12:36.900 --> 00:12:38.140
I'm sorry. I know it's the same thing.

00:12:38.260 --> 00:12:42.900
I prefer the open base. I'd rather tell them, whoever can, go to second.

00:12:43.160 --> 00:12:44.380
You know? I like that.

00:12:44.760 --> 00:12:50.340
I appreciate that more than saying, you, the man on first, start running to your nearest base.

00:12:51.160 --> 00:12:52.520
I mean, I get it either way.

00:12:52.680 --> 00:12:57.020
I just feel like the more I thought about it, I'm like, well, it really is the same thing.

00:12:57.420 --> 00:12:58.920
It's just kind of a preference thing.

00:12:59.040 --> 00:13:01.200
So for some reason, this felt easier to me.

00:13:01.300 --> 00:13:05.240
I couldn't remember ever if it was B or A, though.

00:13:05.360 --> 00:13:06.620
Yeah, exactly. So I just hit both.

00:13:06.980 --> 00:13:07.960
I just hit both.

00:13:08.480 --> 00:13:10.040
That's both retreat and advance.

00:13:10.040 --> 00:13:16.460
But as long as you're designating to an open position, it seemed to always advance.

00:13:16.780 --> 00:13:17.760
I see. Yeah.

00:13:18.180 --> 00:13:20.460
It didn't confuse any of my players.

00:13:20.700 --> 00:13:28.720
I will say that in this game, they improve the AI a bit so that they won't just stop running when they have an...

00:13:28.720 --> 00:13:35.860
I mean, they still are way more conservative than they should be, but they won't just stop running after you first touch the ball.

00:13:35.860 --> 00:13:41.200
If they can very feasibly make it to the next base, they probably will.

00:13:41.480 --> 00:13:46.180
And I appreciated that because I couldn't cheese the AI as much anymore.

00:13:46.320 --> 00:13:51.720
Right. I thought the same thing, actually, a couple times I got into a little hot box running between second and third or whatever.

00:13:51.920 --> 00:13:57.400
There were a couple times where I was like, I bet I could kind of just trick them into letting me get closer and closer to third.

00:13:57.720 --> 00:13:59.140
No, they're pretty smart about it.

00:13:59.180 --> 00:14:00.300
They'll hold the ball. Yeah.

00:14:00.380 --> 00:14:05.160
I thought I could just, no matter what, if I was facing second base, they would throw to second base.

00:14:05.160 --> 00:14:09.020
So I tested it a couple times, and now the AI functions well there.

00:14:09.360 --> 00:14:15.360
Another weird thing, and I don't know if I attribute this to the speed of running that they've given the players or a fielding mechanic,

00:14:15.500 --> 00:14:24.160
but it's really strange to see a situation where a ball can be like a ground ball to shortstop and the man on first can be safe.

00:14:24.160 --> 00:14:26.680
That's just never really going to happen in a game of baseball.

00:14:26.980 --> 00:14:31.120
Something about the measurements of the baseball diamond in this game are off.

00:14:31.820 --> 00:14:34.480
If you want to start talking about fielding, then.

00:14:34.480 --> 00:14:35.680
We can move over to fielding, yeah.

00:14:35.740 --> 00:14:40.520
Because, again, I don't know if that's just that they made the runners too fast or if the fielding is just a little chaotic.

00:14:40.620 --> 00:14:48.900
It's definitely an improvement, though, because in the first game, the speed of the ball was so – well, I actually don't remember if this was the first.

00:14:49.120 --> 00:14:50.220
I think it was the last game.

00:14:50.340 --> 00:14:52.160
Major League Baseball, yeah.

00:14:52.160 --> 00:15:00.020
The speed of the ball in there was so variable, and depending on if it was moving on the X or the Y axis could change significantly.

00:15:00.440 --> 00:15:06.220
But I noticed that in this game, I actually did have more realistic plays.

00:15:06.440 --> 00:15:11.860
Like, I did end up executing a double play at one point, which I never did in Major League Baseball.

00:15:12.060 --> 00:15:17.840
Right. Yeah.

00:15:18.660 --> 00:15:21.140
I was able to pick a couple guys off that way.

00:15:21.320 --> 00:15:22.840
So this is definitely better.

00:15:23.140 --> 00:15:26.000
This is an improvement to the fielding in Major League Baseball.

00:15:26.020 --> 00:15:38.740
Yeah. I think if I hadn't played Major League Baseball, I would be a little more annoyed with this one because I do feel like the ball – if a grounder is hit into the field, it is just so fast, you can't catch up to it.

00:15:38.740 --> 00:15:41.360
I mean, like almost unrealistically so.

00:15:41.620 --> 00:15:45.740
But then when you throw the ball, it's a lot slower than I feel like it should be.

00:15:45.860 --> 00:15:51.680
But it's so much less noticeable when I compare it to Major League Baseball, which it was – that was the case tenfold.

00:15:51.900 --> 00:15:54.160
Yeah. Like, here I was like, it wasn't too bad.

00:15:54.160 --> 00:15:57.620
I would probably have been annoyed with it if I wasn't comparing it to one that was much worse.

00:15:57.920 --> 00:16:05.280
And the game also has both the possibility for errors based on, like, you know, dropping the ball when you try to catch it.

00:16:05.280 --> 00:16:09.240
And it can, like, bop you in the chest and fall down, which I thought was cute.

00:16:09.440 --> 00:16:15.080
But there's also – and I didn't experience this when I was playing, but I did see the computer do it.

00:16:15.180 --> 00:16:17.140
There's a possibility for throwing errors.

00:16:17.740 --> 00:16:18.920
I – okay.

00:16:19.220 --> 00:16:20.760
Like, they legit –

00:16:20.760 --> 00:16:22.540
Yes. Like, they threw it to first.

00:16:22.800 --> 00:16:23.820
Nobody was there.

00:16:23.820 --> 00:16:26.080
So it just went off out into the distance.

00:16:26.080 --> 00:16:27.600
I did have that happen to me as well.

00:16:27.680 --> 00:16:31.060
That's a real thing that does happen in Major League Baseball.

00:16:31.060 --> 00:16:39.200
So, like, I thought that was pretty – like, you know, a pretty neat touch to actually – because we've seen everyone tries to do the box score and have the error thing.

00:16:39.280 --> 00:16:42.940
But there was no real way to figure out, like, what are you calculating as an error?

00:16:43.040 --> 00:16:47.140
And here it's like, okay, at least there's, like, some kind of basic reasoning to it.

00:16:48.140 --> 00:16:54.260
Some things that they didn't improve, though, I would have preferred – and, again, these aren't the same developers, right?

00:16:54.360 --> 00:16:55.220
They're not. No. Okay.

00:16:55.220 --> 00:16:58.140
No, no, no. RBI Baseball is tangent.

00:16:58.140 --> 00:17:02.100
Didn't this actually release first in Japan or wherever?

00:17:02.280 --> 00:17:03.780
Yes, yeah, yeah. We'll get into that.

00:17:03.840 --> 00:17:08.100
I just want to make sure – I don't think that that was – that this was copying that game.

00:17:08.340 --> 00:17:11.240
Regardless, somebody stole somebody's code for the most of this.

00:17:13.080 --> 00:17:26.260
But still, there is no cursor pointing to where your outfielders are because there are so many fly balls that, you know, you get that boop, and I'm just like, okay, where's my guys?

00:17:26.260 --> 00:17:33.520
I know that they're always in the same position to start, but I don't have the muscle memory to know exactly where I have to go.

00:17:33.520 --> 00:17:35.380
And there's no visual reference for them at that point.

00:17:35.500 --> 00:17:40.060
You're seeing the fly ball, and you know that that's a big fly ball, and that it's going to translate to the outfield.

00:17:40.240 --> 00:17:44.720
But you have to already be running towards the ball in most situations to catch it.

00:17:44.860 --> 00:17:48.220
But if you can't see your fielder, it's really hard to control them.

00:17:48.220 --> 00:17:57.620
And I'd add to that that I think it's even worse with the pop-ups because the ball, regardless, it can't show you the actual Z-axis.

00:17:58.040 --> 00:18:00.140
Yeah. So, like – It's an audio Z-axis.

00:18:00.200 --> 00:18:03.380
But, like, yeah. But, like, sometimes the ball is, like – it's like, oh, I got some time.

00:18:03.400 --> 00:18:04.680
I can make that. And then it just drops.

00:18:04.900 --> 00:18:09.400
So, like, I'm looking at it, and I'm like, all right, my outfielder, I finally see him.

00:18:09.580 --> 00:18:11.060
You know, we're going to go a little further back.

00:18:11.060 --> 00:18:12.760
And then it drops, like, in front of us already.

00:18:13.000 --> 00:18:20.280
Yeah. You know, it's – there should have been – and I know that this sounds cheap, but this would have been the best way to communicate it.

00:18:20.480 --> 00:18:29.780
There should have been a circle showing you in pop-up situations where you had to be, and you had to just make sure that you got to that circle to make the catch.

00:18:29.920 --> 00:18:32.380
Well, it does have a shadow, but –

00:18:32.380 --> 00:18:37.820
Yeah, but I meant, like, more of, like, a projected circle of where you need to go, not the shadow of the ball.

00:18:37.840 --> 00:18:39.100
Oh, I see. So, a target.

00:18:39.360 --> 00:18:40.260
Yeah, like a target.

00:18:40.260 --> 00:18:43.800
And I was like, all right, so if you go here – and maybe that's something you could have turned on and off.

00:18:43.840 --> 00:18:44.940
Who knows? But, like –

00:18:44.940 --> 00:18:47.100
You've got to go a couple more generations for that.

00:18:47.200 --> 00:18:59.460
Right, right. I just think that, like, that was missing here because so often the computers are just, like, effortlessly already there when it finally moves over to the outfield.

00:18:59.840 --> 00:19:09.660
And for most players, it's a situation where, other than muscle memory, you might find out that you've been going in the wrong direction, and now you have to steer the other way.

00:19:09.660 --> 00:19:17.860
Yeah, I just – it's weird that the show and probably games before it, because there was a good decade where I didn't play any baseball video games.

00:19:18.980 --> 00:19:28.580
But it's just such an elegant solution of just having a fake shadow of the ball that expands and contracts relative to how high it is.

00:19:28.800 --> 00:19:34.780
Yes. So, I mean, I'm sure that that's technically possible on the NES, but maybe it just hasn't been thought of yet.

00:19:35.140 --> 00:19:41.460
Yeah. And the audio cue is nice, but I don't think it's, like – I don't think it's working as well as it should.

00:19:41.700 --> 00:19:47.840
Yeah. Another thing with fielding that was cool was that there was an option to run to the base just by pressing B.

00:19:48.120 --> 00:19:58.260
Mm-hmm. And I thought that was a nice touch because sometimes you would be the first baseman going off the base and just picking it up,

00:19:58.320 --> 00:20:02.100
but then you didn't want to wait for someone else to step there or just where you had to throw it.

00:20:02.180 --> 00:20:03.340
And you can throw it to nobody.

00:20:03.560 --> 00:20:07.600
Mm-hmm. So being able to run to it was nice, and I thought that was an extra bonus.

00:20:08.040 --> 00:20:11.320
Yeah. So I think that covers everything for fielding for me.

00:20:11.500 --> 00:20:13.720
Anything else from you guys? I don't think so.

00:20:13.780 --> 00:20:22.560
Probably move on to probably the biggest aspect of the game, pitching, because this is where you're the most in control of your fate.

00:20:23.040 --> 00:20:24.720
So how did we feel about pitching?

00:20:26.540 --> 00:20:32.460
I thought that this was a little bit more chaotic than the previous – than Major League Baseball.

00:20:32.460 --> 00:20:39.120
I thought in Major League Baseball I had a lot more of a fine control over where the ball was going.

00:20:39.440 --> 00:20:45.660
In this one, it was more of just sort of like a – you can kind of nudge it in a direction that you want.

00:20:46.560 --> 00:20:54.640
And that's actually, I think, a good thing, though, because, you know, pitches aren't as precise as, I guess, we are led to believe.

00:20:55.800 --> 00:21:05.840
Yeah. And I think it was – I don't remember if Major League Baseball – for some reason, I feel like Major League Baseball lets you choose between four pitches, and this one just lets you choose between two.

00:21:06.600 --> 00:21:14.000
Well, it's – I think it's, you know, it depends on how long you hold the pitch button for how fast it is.

00:21:15.240 --> 00:21:19.720
And then you do the aftertouch thing again with where you want it to go.

00:21:20.060 --> 00:21:25.040
Right. And the aftertouch thing didn't work as well, as Sean already said.

00:21:25.380 --> 00:21:33.100
But for the most part, the AI would be able to play along with you, too, and, like, would swing sometimes at obvious balls.

00:21:33.300 --> 00:21:34.620
It would wait on strikes.

00:21:34.780 --> 00:21:39.620
You know, it's not like if you pitched in the strike zone that you were guaranteed that they were going to make contact with you.

00:21:39.720 --> 00:21:41.040
They weren't superhumans.

00:21:41.040 --> 00:21:47.960
Yeah, I appreciated that because a lot of times – I think we've seen other games, not necessarily a baseball game, but other games that were, like, the AI is just too –

00:21:47.960 --> 00:21:48.880
Like wrestling games. Yeah.

00:21:49.160 --> 00:21:50.780
The AI is just too –

00:21:50.780 --> 00:21:56.720
Good at the game. Yeah, it's just, like, they didn't stop the AI from being able to use all its information that it has.

00:21:57.440 --> 00:21:58.960
So that was nice.

00:21:58.980 --> 00:22:08.580
But I did, you know, just to weigh in on the little aftertouch thing is that I did have a hard time even telling, like, when is this working and when is this not when I do this?

00:22:08.580 --> 00:22:11.140
You know, it was hard to, like, really see the effects of that.

00:22:11.420 --> 00:22:17.940
Yeah. And there is some pitchers, depending on how good they are or unique they are, they'll pitch a knuckleball.

00:22:18.100 --> 00:22:23.920
Yeah. And a sinker ball, which I think were cool, too, to have that much control over where the pitch was going.

00:22:24.120 --> 00:22:30.400
The knuckleball, if you're versing a pitcher who has that, it's very hard to read, and you'll most likely just swing.

00:22:30.400 --> 00:22:37.120
Yeah. I also – because when my pitcher was throwing knuckleballs, I was a terrible pitcher.

00:22:37.140 --> 00:22:38.520
I didn't know what I was even doing.

00:22:38.820 --> 00:22:40.780
So it works both ways.

00:22:41.640 --> 00:22:44.480
And did you guys notice anything about pitcher stamina?

00:22:44.500 --> 00:22:46.140
Because it is in the game.

00:22:46.280 --> 00:22:47.180
Yeah, I did notice the –

00:22:47.180 --> 00:22:50.620
But it's hard for, like, people playing this for the first time to pick up on it.

00:22:50.620 --> 00:22:56.680
I actually – this is one of the few games that I did play as a kid, and I remember very little about it.

00:22:56.720 --> 00:22:59.160
I thought I was going to turn it on and be very nostalgic about it.

00:22:59.220 --> 00:23:00.680
I almost don't recognize it.

00:23:00.760 --> 00:23:05.060
But I do remember having to change pitchers after a while because they get tired.

00:23:05.340 --> 00:23:10.560
I didn't really notice the actual difference in, like, strength.

00:23:10.940 --> 00:23:14.420
But I guess I'd have to play it some more.

00:23:14.520 --> 00:23:21.660
I don't know. Yeah, well, because the manual chooses to include this more, and it's definitely in the game.

00:23:21.800 --> 00:23:26.840
I'm not choosing to – I'm not saying, like, oh, they – because keep in mind, we haven't really addressed this yet,

00:23:26.880 --> 00:23:32.360
but Major League Baseball was a game that let you, like, really get focused on being the manager.

00:23:32.620 --> 00:23:35.180
You know, before the game, you had the whole lineup.

00:23:35.180 --> 00:23:39.880
You had to pick your lineup of an entire player's, choose their batting order and stuff like that.

00:23:40.000 --> 00:23:43.180
In this one, it's already kind of, like, figured out for you.

00:23:43.260 --> 00:23:44.200
Just pick your starter.

00:23:44.200 --> 00:23:46.900
But it was much more involved in Major League Baseball.

00:23:47.040 --> 00:23:49.120
But the same stats are still here.

00:23:49.240 --> 00:23:55.320
You just kind of have to go to, like, the manual to figure that stuff out because they even have, like, for every single team,

00:23:55.320 --> 00:23:57.760
they have, like, contact ratings.

00:23:58.480 --> 00:24:00.540
They have stamina ratings for them.

00:24:00.740 --> 00:24:05.580
They have, like, you know, the amount of home runs that they have correlates to, like,

00:24:05.680 --> 00:24:11.780
if they're more likely to hit a ball, like, out of the park and if they have a better average and, you know, stuff like that.

00:24:11.900 --> 00:24:12.980
So it's all there.

00:24:12.980 --> 00:24:20.280
I just wonder, like, to people who didn't play this game to death, you know, when it came out and, like, played it for years and years,

00:24:20.780 --> 00:24:21.980
how much would you notice that?

00:24:22.080 --> 00:24:30.760
Because I didn't really care who was at bat as much as I might have cared in Major League Baseball where they put the emphasis on the stats, you know?

00:24:30.880 --> 00:24:32.060
So you actually cared a little more.

00:24:32.140 --> 00:24:36.140
In this one, I never, like, I never substituted.

00:24:36.240 --> 00:24:37.480
And that's something you can do in this game.

00:24:37.600 --> 00:24:39.180
Yeah. It's hidden, you know?

00:24:39.360 --> 00:24:40.220
Like, they don't really.

00:24:40.300 --> 00:24:41.320
Well, it's also, it's in the manual.

00:24:41.420 --> 00:24:45.920
No, it's in the manual, but it meant more, like, you could go the whole game without doing it.

00:24:46.140 --> 00:24:48.000
And it requires a couple of extra press.

00:24:48.140 --> 00:24:49.620
You have to pause and then hit buttons.

00:24:49.620 --> 00:24:54.540
I think, yeah, I think it makes it, it actually makes it possible to casually play this game.

00:24:54.680 --> 00:24:56.340
I feel like, like, we all kind of did.

00:24:56.520 --> 00:24:57.760
Or to get really into it.

00:24:57.840 --> 00:25:03.700
And especially if you're playing against someone, get really into it and, like, kind of focus on the more minute details like that.

00:25:04.220 --> 00:25:05.660
Which is nice. Yep.

00:25:06.760 --> 00:25:11.340
But, and, you know, I've already, we've already touched on this with the way the home runs go and stuff.

00:25:11.540 --> 00:25:14.180
But, again, like, presentation is lacking.

00:25:14.320 --> 00:25:15.560
The music's not great either here.

00:25:15.680 --> 00:25:17.360
So, again, I'm missing this whole, like.

00:25:17.480 --> 00:25:24.740
It also has a weird rule change thing where, like, it's like Little League rules with a 10, it's got a 10 run mercy rule.

00:25:25.520 --> 00:25:26.480
Wow. Okay.

00:25:26.600 --> 00:25:27.440
That I didn't notice.

00:25:27.560 --> 00:25:28.540
Yeah, I did notice that.

00:25:28.760 --> 00:25:29.960
I didn't get 10 runs.

00:25:30.060 --> 00:25:34.520
Yeah, I had a game that ended in the seventh inning because of that.

00:25:34.520 --> 00:25:44.140
Yeah, I actually, when I was done playing, we haven't talked about this yet, but I went to the watch feature and let it play in the background because there's a feature in here where it's like, like watching a real baseball game.

00:25:44.460 --> 00:25:52.320
On TV. But I let a couple of them play and one of them, both of them are really high scoring games, but one of them was mercy like that.

00:25:52.520 --> 00:25:58.140
It was like, one of them was like 28 to 22 and the other one was like 18 to, you know, 12 or whatever.

00:25:58.920 --> 00:26:01.320
It's a 10 run mercy rule from wherever.

00:26:01.560 --> 00:26:03.220
It's not the first one to 10 or anything.

00:26:03.220 --> 00:26:04.440
No, no. If you're 10 ahead.

00:26:04.480 --> 00:26:05.520
If you're 10 ahead, yeah.

00:26:05.860 --> 00:26:10.220
Okay, that makes sense. Hey, I guess, I don't even know who that's serving, though.

00:26:10.420 --> 00:26:14.900
I don't know. I guess your younger brother that doesn't want to get beat that bad.

00:26:15.040 --> 00:26:22.200
Right, right. And just throwing this out here, I don't know if you guys did this, but I seem to do this all the time in baseball games, and I definitely did it here.

00:26:22.500 --> 00:26:26.440
Did anyone else just throw to first base all the time after a play was over?

00:26:26.440 --> 00:26:34.760
Or, you know, like you're hitting A to like, you're hitting A to get on, like bring me back to the batting scenario or bring me back to the pitching scenario.

00:26:35.060 --> 00:26:39.740
And instead, it just throws it back to first base because the thing's like, oh, that's what you want to do.

00:26:40.040 --> 00:26:41.500
You're trying to pick them off. Yeah, no one's there, but sure.

00:26:42.160 --> 00:26:45.680
And so I constantly would, because there's such a long pause there.

00:26:45.860 --> 00:26:47.480
Yeah. It's like the only slow part of the game.

00:26:47.600 --> 00:26:49.040
The rest of the game moves pretty fast.

00:26:49.040 --> 00:26:53.200
I didn't actually end up doing that compulsively, but I can see how somebody could.

00:26:53.600 --> 00:26:59.140
That's funny. So after you finish the game, you're greeted with a nice newspaper ending.

00:27:01.400 --> 00:27:09.100
Yeah, a lot of flaws, but also a nice, you know, blank wins, and it gives you the box score.

00:27:09.320 --> 00:27:13.220
It's almost something you would want to like, you know, take a picture of if you were proud of it, you know?

00:27:13.320 --> 00:27:14.620
Did you do that, Mike? No, I didn't.

00:27:14.780 --> 00:27:22.160
Oh, okay. But I just thought, like, it's a nice extra touch to, like, end a game rather than just be like, let's show the scoreboard.

00:27:22.300 --> 00:27:23.920
Like, no, read about it in the paper.

00:27:39.940 --> 00:27:42.380
One thing I don't want to gloss over is the music.

00:27:42.400 --> 00:27:44.300
You mentioned for a second that it wasn't that great.

00:27:44.300 --> 00:27:45.940
I think that is an understatement.

00:27:46.180 --> 00:27:50.920
I thought this, and I usually, like, I'm usually pretty forgiving on a lot of music particularly.

00:27:51.100 --> 00:27:53.880
I thought this music was just, it was the worst part about the game.

00:27:54.080 --> 00:27:56.640
I mean, it was, it was just, like, one tone.

00:27:56.860 --> 00:27:58.860
There were no, it was just, like, do, do, do.

00:27:58.940 --> 00:28:05.480
I can't, I don't remember what the tune actually was, but it was all just, like, every note was one tone, and it was just insanely repetitive.

00:28:05.800 --> 00:28:08.660
I don't remember that from my childhood, it being so repetitive.

00:28:09.100 --> 00:28:16.320
Wow. I mean, I guess I kind of forgot about it because it was just, I knew what I was playing.

00:28:16.420 --> 00:28:19.900
I don't know. Yeah. It actually, for me, usually I don't get, like, particularly annoyed with music.

00:28:20.000 --> 00:28:22.840
This one, like, I got kind of, like, kind of annoyed with it.

00:28:22.840 --> 00:28:23.640
I didn't sound off.

00:28:23.820 --> 00:28:24.640
Yeah. That's smart.

00:28:24.680 --> 00:28:29.020
I couldn't, I couldn't. See, Mike, that's why you didn't know how high these things were.

00:28:29.240 --> 00:28:30.320
Right, right, right. That's true.

00:28:30.440 --> 00:28:31.600
Okay. I've been found.

00:28:31.800 --> 00:28:33.040
I've been found out.

00:28:33.040 --> 00:28:34.500
You have to play the game correctly.

00:28:35.100 --> 00:28:40.800
One thing we have not talked about at all, and it does relate to this game, but I figure

00:28:40.800 --> 00:28:43.160
I'll save this for a later episode, but it needs to be acknowledged.

00:28:43.760 --> 00:28:49.720
I said Tenjin made this game, and there's a long, like, history of this game that we'll

00:28:49.720 --> 00:28:51.040
get into in the sequels and spin-offs section.

00:28:51.040 --> 00:28:56.200
But, yes, this is the very first NES Black Cart game.

00:28:56.660 --> 00:29:03.580
Black Cart. An unlicensed NES game that Tenjin made on their own, saying, like, hey, we're going

00:29:03.580 --> 00:29:04.540
to put this to market.

00:29:04.540 --> 00:29:08.460
Like, we don't care if Nintendo approves of it or not, without their quality seal and everything.

00:29:08.480 --> 00:29:10.860
Are we going to get arrested?

00:29:11.200 --> 00:29:14.900
No, no, no, because the reason why I didn't bring it up in this episode, I didn't talk

00:29:14.900 --> 00:29:19.320
about that whole fiasco, because they also released a licensed one.

00:29:19.320 --> 00:29:21.240
And so we are talking about the licensed version.

00:29:21.400 --> 00:29:22.900
There's no difference, but it is worth noting.

00:29:22.900 --> 00:29:24.540
But when was the licensed version released?

00:29:24.780 --> 00:29:26.780
The licensed, well, I would assume the same time.

00:29:26.880 --> 00:29:28.740
I would assume that the licensed version maybe even came first.

00:29:29.520 --> 00:29:30.580
I'm confused now.

00:29:30.700 --> 00:29:37.300
Yeah, why would they? Well, because eventually they wanted to muscle in their power over, basically they wanted

00:29:37.300 --> 00:29:41.540
to have control over the rules that Nintendo was enforcing on the NES.

00:29:41.760 --> 00:29:43.000
We're going to, like, get into it now here.

00:29:43.120 --> 00:29:44.820
Let's do it. Basically, that's the problem.

00:29:44.940 --> 00:29:47.020
The licensed one was on, just, sorry to interrupt.

00:29:47.100 --> 00:29:48.700
The licensed one was on a normal gray cart.

00:29:48.700 --> 00:29:50.740
Okay, I had the other one when I was a kid.

00:29:51.060 --> 00:29:54.520
Yes, and most people did because they were selling them cheaper because they were able

00:29:54.520 --> 00:29:59.380
to. They figured out a way to make them work on any NES.

00:29:59.960 --> 00:30:03.660
And on top of that, the reason why they did it is because Nintendo had very strict rules

00:30:03.660 --> 00:30:06.180
on how many games you could put out in a year.

00:30:06.400 --> 00:30:11.580
And that's why, like, other companies like Capcom would go on to make, like, other smaller

00:30:11.580 --> 00:30:14.040
developers and, like, be under those names.

00:30:14.040 --> 00:30:16.660
Mm-hmm. And Tenjin was like, no, we're not doing that.

00:30:16.800 --> 00:30:18.320
Like, Tenjin was a subsidiary of Atari.

00:30:18.800 --> 00:30:21.480
And so that just wasn't going to happen.

00:30:21.480 --> 00:30:23.160
So instead, they started selling the games.

00:30:23.280 --> 00:30:29.300
And Nintendo said to Toys R Us, to every major video game distributor at the time, you put

00:30:29.300 --> 00:30:31.500
those games in the store, you're not getting Nintendo games.

00:30:32.180 --> 00:30:36.760
So you can only guess who won that fight.

00:30:37.580 --> 00:30:42.840
Right. Now Atari is making hotels for people to be in.

00:30:42.920 --> 00:30:43.920
Yeah, to live in.

00:30:45.800 --> 00:30:48.720
For a night. Book it in your own experience.

00:30:49.000 --> 00:30:50.200
I thought you were describing what hotels are.

00:30:50.500 --> 00:30:52.580
Yeah, well, we used to have to describe everything in the show.

00:30:53.260 --> 00:30:57.420
For those of you that do know this whole story with Tenjin, obviously that was very rushed,

00:30:57.440 --> 00:30:59.200
so don't get upset about that.

00:30:59.280 --> 00:31:00.320
But I think that's a good overview.

00:31:00.580 --> 00:31:03.080
It sounds like Tenjin is the hero of the working class.

00:31:03.380 --> 00:31:05.560
Well, you know, some people might say that.

00:31:05.760 --> 00:31:07.480
Other people might think, how dare they?

00:31:08.840 --> 00:31:12.860
But we'll get into that more in depth when we actually get to our first unlicensed NES game.

00:31:13.640 --> 00:31:20.160
In the meantime, I'm going to cover a thing that we usually don't cover in Nostalgia,

00:31:20.180 --> 00:31:27.760
and that is that I stumbled upon a fan website for RBI Baseball that was just so beautiful

00:31:27.760 --> 00:31:35.100
and so well taken care of and still has a strong community forum that posts as regularly as yesterday,

00:31:36.020 --> 00:31:38.080
February 4th, 2020.

00:31:38.720 --> 00:31:40.160
Okay? It's called...

00:31:40.160 --> 00:31:41.400
It's called 2020, Mike.

00:31:42.300 --> 00:31:43.400
2020? 2020.

00:31:43.640 --> 00:31:44.880
What did I say? 2020.

00:31:45.220 --> 00:31:46.460
Oh, my God. I was one of those people.

